[{"id":"VRCE-4-21-1","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":1,"english":"Thereupon beholding Tārā fallen (on the ground) like to a star dropt down from the sky, Hanumān, the lord of monkey-herds, consoled her again and again.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.738,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021001","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"624b2da257d981eb96623cad43dbc838ab2ab3b34aab75b85f32dd35e51e7a48","compact":"6bce4dcedfbc226a67e35edbd7298e6edd11aa7ae8e329666d99d73b3d5b26db","nasal":"2a478d290bb02e7851a93523c06f4ba6cdd9e422584fac8df083f29aad1491db"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-2","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":2,"english":"Animals unagitated, attain to happiness or misery, as an outcome of their actions performed (in their previous existence) by merits or demerits.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021002","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"20b24e5fde032eace16aa4bd4d2674bf33c9e92a9b8fd0808be393502c56fd9b","compact":"ed53b7f12126b6699cffb654763381998a2c366ac449408631f9afb77dab3734","nasal":"c4464a635e0ca0a46493efcf9331f1e68de6579c0f75a1e7536e9d5c1a47678e"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-3","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":3,"english":"Why did you mourn for others, yourself being an object of mourning? Why did you feel commiseration for the poor, yourself being poorly? In this body like to a bubble who is there who mourneth for another?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.886,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021003","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d7e7901160d78ca41d2c738152ca99ebd477fefc0fe3c3d22b9a6f11d9d9946c","compact":"165d2adfcdc196c96cb841a7323d6cc4f4e071b6145839474e592762f3b13dc5","nasal":"69d61c6c8e0504085c9bd47ee2dc27b35e97e182f8e5ff38fdb50cc7dcf845da"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-4","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":4,"english":"It behoveth you now, O you having your son alive, to look after prince Angada and think of those duties which remain to be executed by you (after the demise of Vāli).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.889,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021004","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"72ef29ea1b00d58a8e478e21979067ec20d76a5c59455f072649ff3e181fd8a0","compact":"912049604077a127d2c02692839bb8cb083f302b8a2870b469d97754435d0175","nasal":"20a5d16439ef3e47d99b4b6aa430673ee64822497783bf967126c6eb4c6f6279"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-5","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":5,"english":"Do you know that life and death of the animals is very unsettled. It is therefore proper to perform what tends to the welfare of after-life. It does not behove you, O learned damsel, to mourn like others.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021005","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cc80bce962a47511774e04ef7fb307075c315a65bcbaa0b9ee3f308eb2e39d44","compact":"89e5f7bb20a2cabc70ece233b5f64c7551500776724835dc47807137d8e9c118","nasal":"0c5312fe30ccb1c5ea87569b7093c3d02223e47b696ee3e6f38f0c3de60ea8f9"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-6","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":6,"english":"He even, who (while living) was surrounded by thousands of monkeys cherishing hopes, has met his destined end.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.545,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021006","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6ad51a75045c98ae69fced5112a07b6ce197607610206c0223eddbcb223087a0","compact":"c6b552ece1fc62342c62dab7c812f3e1904c0e511535b123f09b6f873fd542a5","nasal":"160e9e5ad84927500be5c6237d749b8e3e0d4c90e65916c261897b10e83a199d"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-7","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":7,"english":"This hero discharged his royal duties in consonance with the sanctions of morality and was gifted with various kingly accomplishments such as conciliation, clarity and forgiveness. He has attained to the land of kings and it becomes you not to mourn for him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021007","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"deeaf30287a0cb21329455f08b0661ad0726280f73175614771400f73d417e3d","compact":"9b54626ea17741f7ed4abee2eedc6a0b6c04b8601114c73ec31b6371ef053376","nasal":"9b54626ea17741f7ed4abee2eedc6a0b6c04b8601114c73ec31b6371ef053376"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-8","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":8,"english":"O blameless damsel, all these foremost of monkeys, this your son Angada, this kingdom of the monkey chief, do belong to you.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.672,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021008","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3b0df3d8ef1615b182f504d77a30178c5734669a540e67211880f5a8323c6290","compact":"7190f12599b98cadc296fac1f3b9904cccf02b5e9187ff5b82330f9c861769cf","nasal":"fd7d1e99ea2c7aeb8509ebb20ef472d7f4ce1d161b373337ce093f0a8b674c43"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-9","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":9,"english":"Do you soon despatch, O passionate lady, these two (Angada and Sugrīva) exercised with grief as they are (for the performance of Vali's funeral rites). And abiding by your commandments let Angada govern the earth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021009","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"05c4a58423f7585eaa4a70c34105f7ffb32e82b171c111aa849f38e10fb99bb1","compact":"8cbb717266bf13d3a991df4ee6193da6190449c6eefc47cee323be1c5801b75d","nasal":"d2d8a7f015bba2644eeee61e2019d4e8bb72f820aa2635b9cc876af96fae0055"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-10","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":10,"english":"Let Angada perform all those ceremonies, which should be gone through by sons according to the Šāstras and which are for the well-being of the king; this is the time for the performance of those funeral rites.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021010","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0c1180a4279959010fd08370092fe1bf81adce7ae234915a7733f3d09c0a144a","compact":"8624f8c7d30a1de3da6a6452a253e587714f88a2974b8c06aef90ed68d44d3ec","nasal":"b2a5ad639d4892dd004d10f0f7b8f8e567a46c66cb020fd27debb2471e348282"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-21-11","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":21,"verse":11,"english":"Performing the funeral ceremonies of the lord of monkeys do you install Angada. And beholding your son thus established on the throne you shall be able to pacify your grief?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-21-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4021011","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94